# Weather theory and services: Aviation Weather Handbook FAA-H-8083-28A; AIM chapter 7; PHAK chapter 12.
# ACS I.C. METAR, TAF and winds-aloft strings are written out in the stem.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Thunderstorm life cycle", "Which event marks the start of the mature stage of a thunderstorm?",
      ["Precipitation begins falling from the cloud base", "The cloud top first reaches the tropopause", "Updrafts form inside a growing cumulus cloud"],
      "A thunderstorm grows in the cumulus stage with updrafts only. Precipitation reaching the ground marks the mature stage, with updrafts and downdrafts side by side. Downdrafts take over in the dissipating stage."),
    S(1, 2, "Icing", "You fly through freezing rain. What does that tell you about the air?",
      ["There is warmer air above you, a temperature inversion", "The air is unstable from the surface up", "The freezing level is far above your altitude"],
      "Freezing rain forms when rain falls from a warm layer above freezing into colder air below. It means warmer air exists at a higher altitude."),
    S(1, 2, "METAR", "METAR KABC 121853Z 28015G28KT 1 1/2SM -SHRA BR BKN007 OVC015 12/11 A2971. What is the ceiling?",
      ["700 feet AGL", "1,500 feet AGL", "15,000 feet AGL"],
      "The ceiling is the lowest broken or overcast layer. BKN007 is a broken layer at 700 feet above ground level."),
    S(1, 2, "TAF", "In a terminal aerodrome forecast, what does TEMPO mean?",
      ["Temporary changes, each lasting less than an hour and in total less than half the period", "A permanent change that occurs gradually over the period", "A 30 percent chance of the stated conditions"],
      "TEMPO forecasts temporary fluctuations expected to last less than an hour each and less than half of the period in total (FAA-H-8083-28A). PROB30 gives a 30 percent probability."),
    S(1, 2, "Convective SIGMET", "Which condition by itself is enough for a convective SIGMET?",
      ["Hail 3/4 inch or larger at the surface", "Moderate icing over a large area", "Ceilings below 1,000 feet over half the area"],
      "Convective SIGMETs cover tornadoes, lines of thunderstorms, embedded thunderstorms, areas of thunderstorms of high coverage, and hail of 3/4 inch or more or surface winds of 50 knots or more. Moderate icing and low ceilings are G-AIRMET items."),
    S(1, 3, "Cloud base", "The surface temperature is 18 degrees C and the dew point is 8 degrees C. Roughly what is the base of cumulus clouds?",
      ["4,000 feet AGL", "2,500 feet AGL", "8,000 feet AGL"],
      "In a convective layer the temperature and dew point converge about 2.5 degrees C per 1,000 feet. A 10 degree spread divided by 2.5 gives about 4,000 feet."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Microbursts", "You fly into a microburst on final. What is the usual order of effects?",
      ["A headwind with a climb, then a downdraft, then a strong tailwind", "A tailwind with a sink, then a headwind with a climb", "A steady downdraft only, with no change in wind"],
      "Entering a microburst gives a performance-increasing headwind, then the core downdraft, then a tailwind that cuts airspeed. Downdrafts can reach about 6,000 feet per minute (AIM 7-1-24)."),
    S(2, 2, "Mountain wave", "Standing lens-shaped clouds over and downwind of a ridge tell you to expect what?",
      ["Mountain wave turbulence", "Smooth, stable air with no wind", "A warm front within the next hour"],
      "Lenticular clouds form at the crests of mountain waves. Rotor clouds below them mark some of the most severe turbulence."),
    S(2, 2, "Frost", "Why must frost be removed from the wings before takeoff?",
      ["It disrupts airflow and can raise the stall speed and cut lift", "It adds so much weight the airplane exceeds maximum takeoff weight", "It blocks the static ports and stops the airspeed indicator"],
      "Even a thin layer of frost roughens the wing surface, spoiling the airflow. That reduces lift and increases drag and stall speed. 14 CFR 91.527 and AC 91-74 say to remove it before flight."),
    S(2, 3, "Winds aloft", "A winds and temperatures aloft forecast for FL340 reads 7525-45. What wind is forecast?",
      ["250 degrees true at 125 knots, minus 45 C", "750 degrees true at 25 knots, minus 45 C", "250 degrees true at 25 knots, minus 45 C"],
      "When speed is 100 to 199 knots, 50 is added to the direction code. Subtract 50 from 75 to get 25 (250 degrees) and add 100 to the speed: 125 knots. Temperatures above 24,000 feet are all negative (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(2, 2, "G-AIRMET", "Which G-AIRMET covers moderate icing and freezing levels?",
      ["Zulu", "Tango", "Sierra"],
      "G-AIRMET Sierra is IFR conditions and mountain obscuration, Tango is turbulence, strong surface winds and low-level wind shear, and Zulu is icing and freezing levels."),
    S(2, 2, "Inversions", "What flying conditions usually come with a low-level temperature inversion?",
      ["Smooth air with poor visibility", "Turbulent air with good visibility", "Showers and gusty surface winds"],
      "An inversion makes the air below it stable. Smoke, haze and fog are trapped near the ground, so the air is smooth but visibility is often poor."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Fronts", "What weather often comes ahead of a fast-moving cold front?",
      ["A squall line of thunderstorms", "Steady drizzle and stratus for several days", "Calm winds and widespread radiation fog"],
      "A fast-moving cold front lifts warm moist air quickly. A squall line of severe thunderstorms can form ahead of the front."),
    S(0, 2, "Fronts", "Which weather is typical when stable moist air rises over a warm front?",
      ["Stratiform clouds with steady precipitation", "Towering cumulus and gusty showers", "Clear skies and good visibility"],
      "Stable air lifted gradually along a shallow warm front produces layered stratus clouds and steady rain or drizzle over a wide area."),
    S(0, 2, "Fog", "Which fog forms when moist air moves over a colder surface?",
      ["Advection fog", "Radiation fog", "Steam fog"],
      "Advection fog forms when moist air is carried over colder ground or water, common along coasts. Radiation fog forms on clear, calm nights; steam fog when cold air moves over warmer water."),
    S(0, 2, "Wind shear", "Where is wind shear likely as a warm front approaches?",
      ["Below the frontal surface, sometimes before the front reaches the airport", "Only above 18,000 feet in the jet stream", "Only after the front has passed the airport"],
      "With a warm front, the shear zone is where the warm air rides over the cold air near the ground. It can exist ahead of the surface front for several hours."),
    S(0, 2, "Turbulence", "Where is clear air turbulence most likely near a jet stream?",
      ["On the polar side, near a sharp curve or trough", "On the equatorial side, far from any trough", "Directly in the jet core at its slowest point"],
      "Clear air turbulence with a jet stream is most common on its polar (low-pressure) side and where the jet curves sharply around a deep trough (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 2, "Icing", "Under which conditions does clear ice most often form?",
      ["Large supercooled drops at temperatures from 0 to about -10 C", "Small drops at temperatures below -20 C", "Dry snow at any temperature below freezing"],
      "Large supercooled drops freeze slowly, flowing back before freezing into clear, hard ice. Small drops freeze on impact as rime."),
    S(0, 2, "Icing", "Which two things are needed for structural icing?",
      ["Visible moisture and an airframe temperature at or below freezing", "Clear air and an outside temperature below -20 C", "High humidity and an outside temperature above freezing"],
      "Structural icing needs visible moisture such as cloud or rain and a temperature at or below 0 degrees C at the airframe."),
    S(0, 2, "METAR", "A METAR includes VV004. What does this mean?",
      ["The sky is obscured and the ceiling is 400 feet", "There are scattered clouds at 400 feet", "The visibility is 4 statute miles"],
      "VV is vertical visibility into an indefinite ceiling, such as fog or heavy snow. VV004 means a 400-foot ceiling."),
    S(0, 2, "Stability", "A cold air mass moves over a much warmer surface. What weather do you expect?",
      ["Cumuliform clouds, showers and good visibility outside them", "Stratus, steady drizzle and poor visibility", "Smooth air and widespread fog"],
      "Heating from below makes the cold air unstable. That brings cumulus clouds, turbulence and showers, with good visibility between them."),
    S(0, 2, "Surface winds", "Why do surface winds cross isobars toward low pressure while winds aloft blow along them?",
      ["Surface friction slows the wind and weakens the Coriolis force", "The Coriolis force is stronger at the surface", "Warm air near the surface always flows toward the low"],
      "Aloft the pressure gradient and Coriolis force balance and the wind follows the isobars. Near the ground, friction slows the wind and reduces the Coriolis force, so the pressure gradient turns it toward the low."),
    S(0, 2, "Weather advisories", "What is a Center Weather Advisory?",
      ["An unscheduled advisory for conditions now or soon to be hazardous, valid up to 2 hours", "A routine forecast issued every 6 hours for each Center area", "A pilot report relayed by a Center controller"],
      "A CWA is an unscheduled short-term warning from a Center Weather Service Unit, often for conditions just below SIGMET levels, valid for up to 2 hours (FAA-H-8083-28A)."),
    S(0, 2, "TAF", "A TAF line contains PROB30 2006/2010 TSRA. What does it mean?",
      ["A 30 percent chance of thunderstorms with rain between 0600Z and 1000Z on the 20th", "Thunderstorms expected for 30 minutes starting at 2006Z", "Thunderstorms certain between 2006Z and 2010Z on the 30th"],
      "PROB30 gives a 30 percent probability for the period that follows, written as day and hour: 2006/2010 is the 20th from 0600Z to 1000Z."),
    S(0, 2, "Sea breeze", "On a sunny afternoon at a coastal airport, which way does the local wind usually blow?",
      ["From the sea toward the land", "From the land toward the sea", "Parallel to the shore in either direction"],
      "Land heats faster than water during the day, so air rises over land and cooler sea air flows inland as a sea breeze. At night the flow reverses to a land breeze."),
]
